1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Hello! We are Team Azoic from Dhaka, Bangladesh—
Daniel Hossain, Fazle Elahi Mahi, Nusrat Jahan Tuli,

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:20,000
Roman Ahmed Tanmoy, Mustakim Musa, and Abir Ahmed Joy.
We are competing in NASA Space Apps 2026 in the challenge: "Field Shift: Adapting Farms with NASA Data".

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Meet Abdul, a smallholder farmer cultivating one hectare in Rajshahi.
Like millions of farmers across Bangladesh, Abdul is locked into a rigid Boro–Fallow–Aman crop rotation.

4
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:37,000
For decades, he has grown water-thirsty Boro rice in the dry winter,
left his land bare during late spring, and planted rainfed Aman in the monsoon.

5
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:45,000
But today, under accelerating climate shifts and falling aquifers,
this traditional cycle is breaking down.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Abdul's farm faces three converging crises.
First, NASA GRACE satellite mascon data reveals that the Barind aquifer is declining at -1.48 centimeters every year.

7
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Deep tubewell pumping for winter Boro rice is exhausting groundwater and driving up energy costs.
Second, NASA MODIS satellite NDVI imagery shows that during the pre-monsoon Kharif-1 season,

8
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:15,000
his fields sit bare for 75 days, leaving topsoil vulnerable to rainstorms and stripping soil organic matter.
A single-season planting advisory cannot fix this. Abdul needs an honest, multi-season crop rotation.

9
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,000
And FieldShift approaches this with strict scientific honesty.
While NASA POWER 0.5° reanalysis initially suggested that pre-monsoon rains had shifted 21 days earlier,

10
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:35,000
our cross-validation with high-resolution NASA GPM IMERG satellite precipitation proved this shift is not confirmed—
p is 0.34, with a median shift of zero days.

11
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Rather than making risky claims, FieldShift adheres to satellite-confirmed IMERG observations
and focuses where adaptation truly works: restructuring the entire crop rotation.

12
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Let's see FieldShift in action. Abdul opens Farm Planning.
His existing Boro–Fallow–Aman rotation is evaluated as the baseline.

13
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Using Mixed Integer Linear Programming solved with PuLP CBC,
FieldShift generates three NASA-informed alternatives.

14
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Abdul selects the Water-Saving Strategy, tailored for the Barind tract.

15
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
For every strategy, FieldShift produces a deterministic five-item card:
why it was recommended, the scientific evidence from 25 years of NASA POWER climatology and SoilGrids,

16
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
the benefits—saving 140 millimeters of irrigation water and fixing 70 kg of biological nitrogen—data gaps, and local risks.
Notice the small "এর মানে কী?" popovers: clicking them explains evapotranspiration,

17
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,000
SMAP root-zone moisture anomalies, and satellite versus ground data in plain Bengali.

18
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Abdul sees exactly when his field is covered across a 12-month calendar strip, replacing bare fallow with restorative mungbean.
And every strategy card ends with our golden rule:

19
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:55,000
"This is for decision support; please verify with your local Sub-Assistant Agriculture Officer (SAAO)".

20
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:05,000
For agronomists and researchers, FieldShift features a 3-Way Decision Comparison Lab.
We benchmark exact MILP optimization against sequential Reinforcement Learning in a custom Gymnasium MDP environment

21
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:15,000
and heuristic domain rules. We disclose transparently that while MILP guarantees mathematical optimality,
RL policies are experimental and require empirical ground calibration.

22
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Most crucially for rural Bangladesh: FieldShift is 100% autonomous offline.
When cellular networks drop in remote villages, every NASA dataset—

23
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:35,000
POWER climatology, GPM IMERG rainfall, SMAP anomalies, MODIS NDVI, and GRACE trends—
remains instantly available from local pre-cached engines. Zero network lag. Zero fabricated numbers.

24
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:45,000
FieldShift does not replace the farmer or the extension officer;
it arms them with a quarter-century of NASA Earth observation science to adapt crop rotations safely, sustainably, and profitably.

25
00:03:45,000 --> 00:04:00,000
We are Team Azoic, and this is FieldShift 2026.
Earth Observations Powering Resilient Bangladesh Agriculture. Thank you!
